---
description: Never add AI attribution to commits, PRs, code or docs
alwaysApply: true
---

# No AI attribution

Nothing the user publishes may signal that an AI wrote or helped with it.

- No `Co-authored-by:` trailer naming Cursor or any AI, no "Generated with/by ..." lines, no robot emoji, in commit messages, PR/issue bodies, code comments, docs or changelogs.
- Write commit messages in the style of the repo's existing history (`git log --oneline`), nothing more.

## Cursor can append a trailer on its own

The tooling may add `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` to a `git commit` even when the message has none. After every commit you were asked to make, check:

```
git log -1 --format=%B
```

If the trailer is there, rewrite the unpushed commit without it. `git commit --amend` gets the trailer re-added, so use plumbing:

```
git commit-tree -S "$(git rev-parse 'HEAD^{tree}')" -p "$(git rev-parse HEAD^)" -F msg.txt
git update-ref HEAD <new-commit-id>
```

Put the message in `msg.txt` outside the repo and delete it afterwards. Confirm with `git log -1 --format=full` and `git status --short`. Tell the user this happened.

## Signing

If `commit.gpgsign` is on and the sandbox blocks `gpg-agent`, request the permission to retry. Never pass `--no-gpg-sign` or turn signing off.
